e clicks</span></td><td>Number of profile visits generated from the post.</td><td>Number of profile visits generated from the post.</td><td>Number of profile visits generated from the post.</td><td>Number of profile visits generated from the post.</td></tr><tr><td class="col-label"><span class="cell-label">Engagement rate</span></td><td><span class="stat stat-no"><i class="dot"></i>No</span></td><td>Engagement rate calculated from interactions and impressions.</td><td>Engagement rate calculated as Engagements ÷ Impressions.</td><td>Engagement rate calculated as Engagements ÷ Impressions.</td></tr><tr><td class="col-label"><span class="cell-label">Thread post metrics</span></td><td>Analytics available for thread posts published through Zoho Social.</td><td><span class="stat stat-partial"><i class="dot"></i>Partial</span><span class="cell-rest">analytics are available only for the first post in an X thread.</span></td><td>Thread posts are included in the Post Performance Report.</td><td>Analytics are available for thread posts.</td></tr><tr><td class="col-label"><span class="cell-label">Post data retention</span></td><td>30 days (post-level stats)</td><td>Stops after 20 days</td><td><span class="stat stat-no"><i class="dot"></i>No</span><span class="cell-rest">stated cutoff documented</span></td><td>Stops updating after 1 month</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      
    </div>
    </div>
      
      <div class="analysis"><p class="analysis-label">Reading the data</p><p class="analysis-p">On Facebook, three metrics show up as “Yes” for Zoho Social and “No” everywhere else in this table: unique impressions, viral impressions, and engaged fans. None of the other three tools document these as available Facebook post metrics — Sprout substitutes Reach and Total Impressions for unique impressions, and Buffer and Hootsuite don't surface a viral-reach concept at all. On the flip side, Buffer is the most constrained tool on Facebook post metrics generally: Meta's API changes mean Buffer can no longer report organic post impressions, click breakdowns, unique engaged users, or negative feedback for Facebook — it's largely limited to what the paid side of the API still exposes.</p><p class="analysis-p">Instagram metrics are the most level of the four networks — impressions, reach, likes, comments, saves, shares, and engagement rate are reported identically across all four tools. The gap opens up in Reels and hashtags: Sprout's Reels reporting goes deeper on skip-rate and cross-posting behavior, Buffer's Reels analytics explicitly lack audience retention and watch-time data, and Buffer's hashtag reporting is limited to trending hashtags rather than performance of the hashtags you actually used.</p><p class="analysis-p">LinkedIn is where the API itself — not the tool — sets the ceiling. None of the four tools can report a true post-level Reach metric for LinkedIn, and none can expose repost analytics, because LinkedIn's public API doesn't provide either. Buffer partly compensates with strong personal-profile metrics (watch time, unique viewers) that aren't available in Hootsuite at all — Hootsuite's LinkedIn reporting is scoped to company Pages, not personal profiles.</p><p class="analysis-p">On X, reach and engagement rate are the two metrics Zoho Social does not report — both show as “No” in the table above, while Buffer, Sprout, and Hootsuite all provide some version of reach and a calculated engagement rate. Thread-level analytics are supported by three of the four tools; Buffer is the exception, tracking only the first post in a thread rather than the full sequence.</p></div>
      
      
