      <div class="analysis"><p class="analysis-label">Reading the data</p><p class="analysis-p">Facebook is where the sharpest asymmetry in this whole report shows up: Zoho Social is the only tool of the four reporting viral vs. non-viral views, while Buffer is the only one that cannot report organic Facebook impressions at all, because Meta's API removed that data for Buffer specifically as documented in its own support content. The two tools also split in the opposite direction on paid reach and paid link clicks — Zoho Social doesn't report either, while Buffer, Sprout, and Hootsuite all do. In other words, Zoho Social's Facebook strength is concentrated on the organic and viral side; its gap is on the paid side.</p><p class="analysis-p">On Instagram, the pattern flips: Zoho Social shows “No” across every row in this table — organic impressions/reach, boosted-post splits, and Stories organic data are all reported by Buffer, Sprout, and Hootsuite but not documented for Zoho Social's Instagram reporting specifically. That's a meaningful gap for any team running paid Instagram alongside organic and trying to see the split inside the tool itself.</p><p class="analysis-p">LinkedIn's organic-vs-sponsored follower chart is the one place Zoho Social includes something as a standard, dedicated feature that Sprout gates behind Premium Analytics and Hootsuite gates behind Advanced Analytics. None of the four tools offer a true post-level organic-vs-sponsored split on LinkedIn beyond a CSV export (Sprout, for Facebook) or profile-level-only organic data (Sprout, for LinkedIn) — this is the one row in the entire organic/paid section where every tool reads “No” for the most granular version of the metric.</p></div>
      
      
